  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.2. Increased capacity of Member States to ensure safe and healthy working conditions</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Government of Eswatini (Cabinet) developed and adopted a gender-responsive National Occupational Safety and Health (OSH) Policy with an accompanying implementation plan, and tool for monitoring and evaluating the Policy in August 2023.  The development of the OSH Policy and its Implementation Plan as well as monitoring tool was driven through a process of stakeholder consultation and social dialogue, under direct leadership of a National Tripartite Committee on Occupational Safety and Health (TCOSH). Operationalization of the OSH Policy commenced before its official adoption by Cabinet through the commissioning of a gap analysis of the national legislation in respect of the ILO conventions C155 and C187, and sensitization of the tripartite constituents on these conventions as a step towards strengthening the national OSH System and governance in line with one of the objectives of the OSH Policy. In October 2021 and April 2022, the ILO convened workshops to sensitize the TCOSH and other key OSH stakeholders on Conventions 155, 161, 187 and 190 in preparation for the drafting of the national Occupational Health and Safety policy. This was to ensure alignment of the OSH Policy with the said conventions. From October 2021 to April 2022, the ILO supported and facilitated workshops for the actual drafting of the National OSH Policy by the TCOSH.  In June 2022, the ILO supported the Ministry of Labour and Social Security to host and facilitate a validation workshop for the National OSH Policy and its implementation plan.  From January 2023 &#x2013; July 2023, the ILO maintained regular contact with the Ministry of Labour and Social Security to advocate for the tabling of the National OSH Policy at Cabinet for adoption.</narrative>
   </description>
