   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Labour Inspectorate of the Ministry of Labour &amp; Social Security developed and adopted a Strategic Compliance Plan (SCP) for the Education Sector in March 2022. The plan assists the labour inspectorate to improve its delivery in promoting compliance in the education sector with various pieces of labour legislation in respect of support staff in schools. The areas of focus which the plan seeks to promote compliance and enforcement of legislation are notably the following: payment of authorised deductions; observance of holiday entitlements; payment during sickness; insurance of employees in schools; maintenance of accurate employment records; and failure to insure employees. The Labour Inspectorate is collaborating with the Ministry of Education and Training to educate and promote compliance with legislation pertaining to working conditions for support staff in Schools. 
The SCP for the Education sector is underpinned by the core elements of labour inspections as defined by Convention No. 81 and No. 129. At the time of this reporting, the strategic compliance plan in Eswatini is well underway with 68% of actions devised in the plan completed.
Through the ILO&#x2019;s support to Eswatini for the development of a SCP for the private security services sector, Eswatini learned the model and applied it in the education sector.  This biennium, Eswatini independently transferred that learning to the selection of a new sector &#x2013; the education sector in March 2022 - in consultation with the social partners.  The SCP in education is evidence of Eswatini&#x2019;s institutionalization of the model. In November 2022, the ILO organized a training workshop for labour inspectors to enhance their capacity to better collect and use evidence during prosecutions. From 5-7 December 2022, the ILO facilitated a training of the inspectorate for the development of social media campaigns to enhance real-time public engagement by raising awareness about the inspectorate&#x2019;s services, disseminating information on labour law reforms, providing compliance guidance, and reaching diverse audiences, including younger workers and those in non-standard employment.  In March 2023, the ILO supported the labour inspectorate to design regional capacity building workshops for school principals, school Chairpersons and support staff focusing on working conditions for support staff. These workshops were held in May 2023, during which a total of 1,600 school staff attended the training in the Hhohho region; 1,000 in Manzini; 700 in the Shiselweni region; and 940 in the Lubombo region.   In March 2023, the ILO facilitated a workshop for the labour inspectorate on stakeholder collaboration in promoting compliance. To this end the workshop identified various stakeholders with whom the Ministry of Labour and Social Security could collaborate and eventually establish formalized agreements to improve labour law compliance.  In May 2023, the inspectorate used the ILO social media for compliance toolkit to launch a media campaign to raise awareness in the education sector on the Regulation of Wages (Schools and Other Educational Institutions Support Staff Industry) Order, 2023.</narrative>
   </description>
